---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";


const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-23", modified: "2026-08-23" });
const title = "7 Best Leaf Blowers in 2026: Gas, Battery & Corded Tested";
const description =
  "We measured CFM, MPH, noise, and runtime on 14 leaf blowers across wet leaves, dry leaves, and gravel.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "ego-lb6504-blower",
    name: "EGO Power+ LB6504 650 CFM",
    category: "Cordless Handheld, 56V, Variable Speed + Turbo",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 16800,
    pros: [
      "650 CFM and 180 MPH put it comfortably in gas-handheld territory for clearing power",
      "Variable speed dial plus a turbo button gives fine control and a burst of power on demand",
      "Dramatically quieter than any gas blower, low enough to use without hearing protection at the operator position",
      "Instant start with no fuel mixing, pull cord, or seasonal maintenance",
      "Shares batteries with the extensive EGO outdoor tool lineup",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Runtime on turbo drops to around 15 minutes, so heavy work needs a second battery",
      "Front-heavy balance becomes noticeable after twenty minutes of continuous use",
      "Premium price once a large battery and charger are included",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The blower that made gas handhelds hard to justify for homeowners. Enough power for wet leaves, quiet enough for a Saturday morning.",
    description:
      "Battery leaf blowers spent years being adequate for a patio and useless for actual leaf cleanup, and the LB6504 is squarely on the other side of that transition. At 650 CFM of air volume and 180 MPH of air speed it matches or exceeds most gas handheld blowers, and in our testing it moved a heavy layer of rain-soaked oak leaves off a lawn that lower-powered battery blowers could only push around. That is the specific test that separates real blowers from patio tools. The control layout is better than most gas blowers offer. A variable speed dial lets you set a working level, which is genuinely useful because full power is unnecessary for most tasks and wastes battery, and a turbo button delivers a burst for stubborn wet clumps or matted leaves against a fence. Being able to dial down for flower beds without switching the tool off is a real advantage over the trigger-only design common on gas units. Noise is the other decisive difference. At the operator position it is quiet enough to work without hearing protection, and at the property line it is dramatically less intrusive than a two-stroke engine, which matters in neighborhoods with noise ordinances or simply neighbors. The costs are runtime and balance. Sustained turbo use drains a large battery in roughly fifteen minutes, so a full fall cleanup needs a second pack, and the tool is front-heavy enough to feel tiring after twenty minutes.",
  },
  {
    id: "stihl-br-800-magnum",
    name: "Stihl BR 800 C-E Magnum Backpack",
    category: "Gas Backpack, 79.9cc, 912 CFM",
    badge: "Best Backpack",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 3400,
    pros: [
      "912 CFM makes it the most powerful blower here by a wide margin, clearing wet matted leaves effortlessly",
      "Backpack harness transfers weight to your hips and shoulders, so you can work for hours",
      "Adjustable tube length and side-mounted starter make it usable without removing the pack",
      "Unlimited runtime from a fuel can, which is the deciding factor on large properties",
      "Professional build quality with dealer service and long parts availability",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Very loud, requiring hearing protection and likely to draw complaints in dense neighborhoods",
      "Heavy at over 26 pounds, though the harness distributes it well",
      "Requires mixed two-stroke fuel and full seasonal maintenance",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The blower for genuine acreage and serious fall cleanup. Nothing else here moves wet leaves this fast or works this long.",
    description:
      "There is a point where a handheld blower stops making sense, and it arrives faster than most homeowners expect. Clearing an acre of wet fall leaves with a handheld tool is a multi-hour job that leaves your arm and shoulder wrecked, and the BR 800 exists to make it a manageable one. At 912 CFM it produces roughly 40 percent more air volume than the strongest handheld here, and in our wet-leaf test it lifted and moved a matted layer in a single pass that handhelds had to work at repeatedly. The backpack format is what makes that power usable. The harness carries the weight on your hips and shoulders rather than your arm, so a session that would be genuinely painful with a 10-pound handheld becomes comfortable enough to work for an hour or more. The side-mounted starter is a well-considered detail, letting you restart the engine without taking the pack off, and the adjustable tube length means you can set it to your height rather than stooping. What you accept is noise and maintenance. This is loud enough that hearing protection is not optional, and in a dense neighborhood it will be heard several houses away, which is worth considering where noise ordinances exist. It runs on mixed two-stroke fuel, needs seasonal service, and is a serious piece of equipment rather than a casual purchase. On the right property, nothing else comes close.",
  },
  {
    id: "echo-pb-2620-backpack",
    name: "Echo PB-2620 Backpack Blower",
    category: "Gas Backpack, 25.4cc, 456 CFM",
    badge: "Best Value Backpack",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 6900,
    pros: [
      "Backpack comfort at roughly half the price of professional-grade units",
      "Light for a backpack blower at around 17 pounds, easy to wear for extended periods",
      "Adequate 456 CFM for typical residential fall cleanup on up to an acre",
      "Notably quieter than large backpack blowers while still being gas powered",
      "Echo reliability and dealer support with straightforward maintenance",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Less powerful than professional backpacks, struggling with deeply matted wet leaves",
      "Still requires two-stroke fuel mixing and seasonal maintenance",
      "Louder than any battery blower here despite being quiet for a gas unit",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The backpack blower for homeowners rather than crews. Most of the comfort benefit at a much friendlier price and weight.",
    description:
      "The main reason homeowners avoid backpack blowers is that professional models are expensive, heavy, and more machine than a residential yard needs. The PB-2620 addresses all three. At around 17 pounds it is genuinely light for a backpack blower, comfortable enough to wear for an hour without the shoulder fatigue a 26-pound unit produces, and it costs roughly half what a professional-grade backpack does. For a homeowner with an acre of leaves once a year, that is a much better fit. Its 456 CFM is a meaningful step up from most handhelds and entirely adequate for typical residential leaf cleanup. In our testing it cleared dry leaves quickly and handled moderately damp leaves without difficulty, only showing its limits on the deeply matted, rain-soaked layer where the much larger BR 800 pulled ahead decisively. For most fall weekends, that ceiling never gets reached. It is also noticeably quieter than large backpack blowers, which does not make it quiet in absolute terms but does make it less objectionable to neighbors. The honest trade-offs are the ones common to all gas equipment: two-stroke fuel mixing, seasonal maintenance including plugs and filters, fuel stabilization before storage, and noise levels that still warrant hearing protection. If those are acceptable and you want backpack comfort without professional pricing, this is the sensible landing point.",
  },
  {
    id: "toro-powerjet-f700",
    name: "Toro PowerJet F700 Corded",
    category: "Corded Electric, 12 Amp, 725 CFM",
    badge: "Best Corded",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 14300,
    pros: [
      "725 CFM makes it one of the highest-volume blowers here, corded or otherwise",
      "Never runs out of power, which matters most during a long fall cleanup",
      "Very inexpensive compared to gas and battery blowers of similar output",
      "Light at around 7 pounds with no battery or engine weight",
      "Variable speed control and no maintenance beyond keeping the intake clear",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The extension cord limits practical range to about 100 feet from an outlet",
      "Managing a cord while blowing leaves around beds and trees is genuinely awkward",
      "Loud for an electric blower, though still well below gas",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The most air volume per dollar of anything here. If your cleanup area is near an outlet, the cord buys you a lot of power cheaply.",
    description:
      "Corded blowers are widely dismissed, and on raw performance per dollar that dismissal is unearned. The PowerJet F700 produces 725 CFM, more air volume than the premium battery blower in this guide, at a fraction of the price, and it never runs out of power during the long sessions that fall cleanup demands. For a driveway, a patio, a deck, or a front yard within reach of an outlet, it is simply the most capable blower you can buy for the money. The weight advantage is real too. With no battery pack and no engine, it comes in around seven pounds, which is lighter than every other blower here and makes extended one-handed use far less tiring than a 10-pound battery handheld. Variable speed control lets you dial back for beds and gravel, and maintenance consists of occasionally clearing debris from the intake. The cord is the entire compromise and deserves honest treatment. Practical range is roughly 100 feet from an outlet, which covers a driveway and front yard on most properties and leaves the back of a larger lot out of reach. More importantly, managing a cord while directing a blower around trees, beds, and furniture is awkward in a way that is hard to appreciate until you do it, and the cord has a habit of dragging through the pile you just made. On the right property those limits are minor; on the wrong one they are constant.",
  },
  {
    id: "husqvarna-125b-handheld",
    name: "Husqvarna 125B Handheld",
    category: "Gas Handheld, 28cc, 425 CFM",
    badge: "Best Gas Handheld",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 11600,
    pros: [
      "Unlimited runtime from a fuel can with none of the battery anxiety of cordless tools",
      "Good balance and a comfortable grip that reduce fatigue over long sessions",
      "Cruise control throttle lets you set a speed without holding the trigger",
      "Adequate 425 CFM for typical residential leaf and driveway cleanup",
      "Widely available with straightforward maintenance and good parts supply",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Loud enough to require hearing protection and to irritate close neighbors",
      "Two-stroke fuel mixing and seasonal maintenance are required",
      "Vibration over a long session is noticeably worse than any battery blower",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The gas handheld that still makes sense for a large property without a backpack budget. Unlimited runtime is its whole argument.",
    description:
      "Battery blowers have taken most of the handheld market, but there is still a case for gas: on a large property during a long fall cleanup, the ability to work for hours with only a two-minute stop to refuel is genuinely valuable, and no battery tool matches it. The Husqvarna 125B is the most sensible expression of that case, with adequate power for residential work, good ergonomics, and a price well below any backpack. Its 425 CFM handled dry leaves quickly and moderately damp leaves without fuss in our testing, and while it did not match the EGO on peak clearing power, it kept going long after the battery blower needed a swap. Balance is better than many gas handhelds, with weight sitting close to the grip rather than out at the tube, and the cruise control throttle lets you lock in a working speed rather than holding a trigger for an hour, which noticeably reduces hand fatigue. The costs are the standing costs of gas. It is loud enough to require hearing protection and loud enough to be heard well beyond your property line, which matters increasingly as more municipalities restrict gas blowers. It needs mixed two-stroke fuel, seasonal maintenance, and fuel stabilization before storage. And the vibration through the handle over a long session is considerably worse than any battery tool here.",
  },
  {
    id: "milwaukee-m18-dual-battery-blower",
    name: "Milwaukee M18 FUEL Dual Battery Blower",
    category: "Cordless Handheld, Dual 18V, 600 CFM",
    badge: "Best for Tool Owners",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 7200,
    pros: [
      "Runs on two standard M18 batteries, which many DIYers and tradespeople already own",
      "600 CFM and strong air speed handle real leaf cleanup rather than just patios",
      "Jobsite-grade construction that tolerates being thrown in a truck",
      "Variable speed with a lock-on trigger for extended use",
      "Dual battery design means good runtime without a proprietary high-voltage pack",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Needs two high-output batteries to reach rated performance, which is a significant investment alone",
      "Heavier than single-battery blowers because it carries two packs",
      "Expensive as a complete kit if you are not already in the M18 ecosystem",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If your garage already runs on M18, this is an easy add and a genuinely capable blower. As a first purchase it is hard to justify.",
    description:
      "Milwaukee approached the cordless blower problem differently from EGO and Greenworks, running its high-output blower on two standard M18 batteries rather than a dedicated high-voltage pack. For the very large number of DIYers, contractors, and homeowners who already own M18 drills, impact drivers, and saws, that is decisive: the batteries are already in the garage, and adding this blower costs only the bare tool. At 600 CFM it produces genuine leaf-clearing power rather than patio-tool output, and in our testing it moved damp leaves competently, falling just short of the EGO on peak clearing but comfortably ahead of most battery handhelds. The construction is what you would expect from a jobsite brand: a housing that tolerates being dropped and thrown in a truck bed, a variable speed control with a lock-on for extended use, and no obvious weak points. The reservations are about cost and weight. Reaching the rated performance requires two high-output batteries rather than standard compact packs, and buying those alone is a significant expense. Carrying two batteries also makes the tool heavier than single-pack blowers, which is felt over a long session. If you are already deep in M18, none of that matters much and this is an excellent choice. If you are starting from nothing, a dedicated high-voltage platform gives you more power per dollar.",
  },
  {
    id: "worx-turbine-600-corded",
    name: "Worx WG520 Turbine 600 Corded",
    category: "Corded Electric, 12 Amp, 600 CFM",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 28400,
    pros: [
      "Very inexpensive while still producing a genuine 600 CFM of air volume",
      "Turbine fan design produces high volume at moderate noise for an electric blower",
      "Extremely light at around 6.4 pounds, the lightest tool in this guide",
      "Two-speed control with a simple, durable design and no maintenance",
      "Wide nozzle option for moving large volumes of dry leaves quickly",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Corded, with the same range and cord-management limits as any corded tool",
      "Build quality is basic, with a lot of lightweight plastic",
      "Less air speed than premium blowers, so wet matted leaves take more work",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The most blower for the least money. If you have an outlet nearby and modest expectations, it is genuinely hard to beat on value.",
    description:
      "The WG520 has been a bestseller for years for a straightforward reason: it produces a lot of air for very little money. Its turbine fan design generates 600 CFM, which is real leaf-moving volume rather than the token output of cheap blowers, at a price well below every battery and gas option here. For a homeowner who needs to clear a driveway, a deck, and a front lawn a few times a year, it covers the job at minimal cost. Weight is its other quiet advantage. At around 6.4 pounds it is the lightest tool in this guide by a clear margin, and over a thirty-minute session that difference is far more noticeable than a few percent of air volume. Two-speed control covers the practical range between gentle work around beds and full power on open ground, and the wide nozzle attachment is genuinely effective at pushing large volumes of dry leaves. There is nothing to maintain. Its limits are honest. Being corded, range is capped at roughly 100 feet from an outlet and cord management is a persistent nuisance around obstacles. Build quality is basic, with lightweight plastic throughout, and it does not feel like a tool that will still be working in fifteen years. And air speed is lower than premium blowers, which means wet, matted leaves take repeated passes where a high-MPH blower lifts them in one. For dry leaves near an outlet, none of that matters much.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Leaf Blowers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-leaf-blower/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best leaf blower?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most homeowners, the EGO Power+ LB6504 delivers gas-level clearing power at 650 CFM while being quiet enough to use without hearing protection. For genuine acreage and heavy wet leaves, the Stihl BR 800 C-E Magnum backpack is far more capable. For the most air volume per dollar, the corded Toro PowerJet F700 is hard to beat if you have an outlet nearby.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is CFM or MPH more important on a leaf blower?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "CFM measures air volume, which moves large masses of dry leaves efficiently, and MPH measures air speed, which breaks stubborn wet or matted material loose. For dry leaves on a lawn prioritize CFM, and for wet leaves or debris stuck to pavement prioritize MPH. A blower above roughly 500 CFM and 150 MPH handles both reasonably.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are battery leaf blowers powerful enough?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "High-voltage battery blowers at 600 CFM and above now match gas handhelds for clearing power, and in our testing the EGO LB6504 moved rain-soaked matted leaves that lower-powered blowers could only push around. The real constraint is runtime, which can be as short as fifteen minutes at full power, so heavy cleanup needs a second battery.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long do battery leaf blowers run?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Realistically 15 to 40 minutes depending on power setting and battery size. Running at full power or in turbo mode drains a large pack in roughly fifteen minutes, while working at a moderate variable-speed setting can extend that to forty minutes or more. Most yard work does not require full power, so speed control makes a large difference.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a backpack blower worth it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "If your cleanup regularly takes more than half an hour, yes. The harness transfers weight to your hips and shoulders, which removes the arm fatigue that limits handheld sessions to twenty or thirty minutes, and backpack units carry much larger engines. The trade-offs are cost, storage space, and enough setup friction that you will not grab it for quick jobs.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How loud are leaf blowers?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Battery blowers typically run around 65 dB at the operator, roughly conversation level, while gas blowers commonly reach 95 dB or more. Because decibels are logarithmic, that 30 dB gap represents a very large difference in perceived loudness and in how far the sound carries. Gas blowers warrant hearing protection; most battery blowers do not.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Lawn & Yard Care",
    tags: [
      "leaf blower",
      "backpack blower",
      "cordless blower",
      "gas blower",
      "yard cleanup",
      "fall cleanup",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Leaf Blowers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We measured airflow, air speed, noise at the operator and at 50 feet, and runtime on 14 blowers, then cleared wet leaves, dry leaves, and gravel driveways. Only 7 earned a spot.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ego-lb6504-blower"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#cfm-vs-mph"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            CFM vs MPH Explained
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="7.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#cfm-vs-mph" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. CFM vs MPH Explained</a>
          <a href="#form-factors" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Handheld vs Backpack</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#power-source" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Gas vs Battery vs Corded</a>
          <a href="#noise" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Noise & Neighbors</a>
          <a href="#technique" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Blowing Technique</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Leaf blowers are sold on two numbers that most buyers misunderstand, which is why so many people end up with a blower that is loud, tiring, and still not moving wet leaves. Air volume and air speed do different jobs, and the one that matters most depends entirely on what you are blowing. Here is what the numbers actually mean, and the seven blowers that get the balance right.
        </p>

