        <!-- SECTION 1: CFM vs MPH: What the Numbers Actually Mean -->
        <div id="cfm-vs-mph" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. CFM vs MPH: What the Numbers Actually Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every leaf blower is marketed with two numbers, and most buyers assume the bigger one is better. They measure completely different things, and which one matters depends on what you are trying to move.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>CFM is cubic feet per minute, the volume of air the blower moves.</strong> Volume is what pushes a large mass of material along the ground. A high-CFM blower moves a wide swath of leaves steadily forward, which is what you want when clearing a lawn covered in dry leaves. Volume is the number that determines how quickly a big job gets done.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>MPH is miles per hour, the speed of the air leaving the nozzle.</strong> Speed is what breaks material loose. A high-MPH blower lifts wet leaves stuck to pavement, blasts pine needles out of gravel, and dislodges matted debris from a corner. Speed is the number that determines whether stubborn material moves at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The two trade off against each other.</strong> For a given engine or motor, a narrow nozzle produces higher speed and lower volume, while a wide nozzle produces higher volume and lower speed. This is why many blowers ship with more than one nozzle, and why the wide nozzle is the one to use for dry leaves on open lawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Newtons of force is the honest combined figure.</strong> Some manufacturers, particularly on professional equipment, publish blowing force in newtons, which combines volume and speed into a single measure of how hard the air stream actually pushes. Where it is available it is the most useful single number for comparing blowers, and it is far harder to inflate through marketing than CFM alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Practical guidance:</strong> for dry leaves on a lawn, prioritize CFM. For wet leaves, pine needles in gravel, or debris stuck to hard surfaces, prioritize MPH. For a blower that has to do both, look for something above roughly 500 CFM and 150 MPH, which is where handheld tools stop being frustrating.
          </p>

