          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Handheld, Backpack, or Walk-Behind
          </h2>

          <p>
            Format determines how long you can comfortably work more than it determines how much air you move, and for fall cleanup, working time is usually the binding constraint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Handheld Blowers</h3>

          <p>
            The default for most homeowners. Light, easy to store, cheap, and perfectly adequate for driveways, patios, decks, and lawns up to roughly half an acre of leaf coverage. The limitation is arm fatigue: holding six to ten pounds out in front of you and directing it precisely gets genuinely tiring after twenty to thirty minutes, and no amount of power compensates.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Backpack Blowers</h3>

          <p>
            The harness transfers weight to your hips and shoulders, which means you can work for an hour or more without the arm fatigue that limits handheld sessions. They also carry much larger engines and produce far more air volume. The costs are price, storage bulk, and the fact that putting one on and taking it off is enough friction that you will not grab it for a two-minute driveway sweep.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Walk-Behind Blowers</h3>

          <p>
            Wheeled units with very large engines, used for genuine acreage and commercial work. They move enormous volumes of material but cannot get into beds, corners, or up against structures, so they complement rather than replace a handheld. Only worth considering above about two acres of heavy leaf coverage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Two-Tool Answer</h3>

          <p>
            Many people with large properties end up with both a backpack for the annual heavy cleanup and a light handheld or corded blower for quick driveway and patio work through the year. If your usage splits that way, buying one tool that compromises on both jobs often satisfies neither.
