        <!-- SECTION 4: Gas, Battery, or Corded -->
        <div id="power-source" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Gas, Battery, or Corded
          </h2>

          <p>
            The power source decision has shifted decisively toward battery for most homeowners in the last several product generations, but there are still clear cases for each.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery</h3>

          <p>
            Modern high-voltage battery blowers at 600 CFM and above genuinely match gas handhelds for clearing power while being dramatically quieter, starting instantly, and requiring no maintenance. The constraint is runtime, which on full power can be as little as fifteen minutes and rarely exceeds forty. For most suburban cleanups that is enough; for a full fall leaf drop on an acre it means owning two batteries.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas</h3>

          <p>
            Gas retains two advantages: unlimited runtime from a fuel can, and access to the very high output backpack units that no battery product matches. If your cleanup takes hours rather than minutes, or if you are moving deeply matted wet leaves across acreage, gas is still the right answer. The costs are noise, two-stroke fuel mixing, vibration, and seasonal maintenance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Corded</h3>

          <p>
            Corded blowers deliver the most air volume per dollar by a wide margin and never run out of power. The cord caps range at about 100 feet from an outlet and is genuinely awkward to manage while directing a blower. Excellent for driveways, patios, decks, and garages; poor for the back of a large property.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Regulatory Angle</h3>

          <p>
            A growing number of municipalities restrict or ban gas leaf blowers, usually on noise grounds and sometimes with seasonal exemptions. If you live in or near a jurisdiction considering such rules, that is worth factoring into a purchase you expect to keep for a decade, since a battery blower is unaffected by them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Noise, Neighbors, and Hearing Protection -->
        <div id="noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Noise, Neighbors, and Hearing Protection
          </h2>

          <p>
            Leaf blowers are among the most complained-about pieces of homeowner equipment, and the difference between models is large enough to matter to your relationship with the people who live near you.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Understand the decibel scale.</strong> Decibels are logarithmic, so a 10 dB increase represents roughly a doubling of perceived loudness. A gas backpack blower at around 95 dB is not marginally louder than a battery handheld at 65 dB, it is dramatically louder in a way that carries much farther and penetrates walls and windows.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Two-stroke engines produce a particularly intrusive sound.</strong> Beyond raw volume, the low-frequency component of a two-stroke engine travels farther and penetrates building materials better than the higher-pitched whine of an electric motor. This is why gas blowers generate complaints that battery blowers of similar measured loudness do not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wear hearing protection with gas equipment.</strong> Sustained exposure above roughly 85 dB causes cumulative hearing damage, and gas blowers exceed that comfortably at the operator position. Hearing loss from yard equipment is gradual, permanent, and entirely preventable with a pair of muffs that cost very little.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check local ordinances before buying gas.</strong> Many municipalities restrict blower use to certain hours, cap allowable decibel levels, or ban gas blowers outright with phased timelines. These rules have expanded steadily, and buying a gas blower into a jurisdiction that is phasing them out is an expensive mistake.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use less power than the tool has.</strong> Variable speed control exists partly for this reason. Most yard work does not need full power, and running at half throttle is meaningfully quieter, uses less battery or fuel, and blows less dust and mulch out of beds. Reaching for full power only when the job requires it is the single easiest courtesy to extend to neighbors.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Neighbor Test</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Before buying a gas blower, consider that your neighbors will hear it for the entire time you use it, and it carries well beyond your property line. A battery blower at 65 dB is roughly conversation level at the operator and largely inaudible three houses away. If you live on a dense street, that difference may matter more to your daily life than fifteen extra minutes of runtime.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Technique That Cuts Cleanup Time in Half -->
        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Technique That Cuts Cleanup Time in Half
          </h2>

          <p>
            Leaf blowing rewards method far more than power. Most people work harder than they need to because of a few habits that are easy to change.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Work with the wind, not against it.</strong> This sounds obvious and is routinely ignored. Check the wind direction before you start and plan your piles downwind. Fighting a breeze doubles the work and undoes progress continuously.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Blow in one direction toward a single destination.</strong> Decide where the leaves are going before you start, then work in consistent parallel passes toward it. The most common inefficiency is blowing leaves in whichever direction is convenient at the moment, which creates scattered piles that then have to be consolidated.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use the nozzle angle, not just the power.</strong> Holding the nozzle at a shallow angle a few inches off the ground moves leaves forward efficiently. Pointing it straight down blasts leaves in all directions and lifts dust and mulch out of beds. Angle does more for control than the speed setting does.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Work in strips and do not chase strays.</strong> Move a manageable band of leaves forward, then step over and move the next band into the same line. Chasing individual escaped leaves costs far more time than picking them up on a final pass.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wet leaves need speed, then volume.</strong> Use a narrow nozzle and high speed to break a matted layer loose, then switch to a wider nozzle to move the freed material efficiently. Trying to move matted wet leaves with volume alone simply pushes the whole mat around.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Blow onto a tarp, not into a pile.</strong> Directing leaves onto a spread tarp rather than a freestanding pile turns the disposal step into dragging one load rather than raking a pile into bags. On a property with a real leaf drop, this saves more time than anything else on this list.
          </p>
        </div>

