            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is CFM or MPH more important on a leaf blower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                CFM measures air volume, which moves large masses of dry leaves efficiently, and MPH measures air speed, which breaks stubborn wet or matted material loose. For dry leaves on a lawn prioritize CFM, and for wet leaves or debris stuck to pavement prioritize MPH. A blower above roughly 500 CFM and 150 MPH handles both reasonably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are battery leaf blowers powerful enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                High-voltage battery blowers at 600 CFM and above now match gas handhelds for clearing power, and in our testing the EGO LB6504 moved rain-soaked matted leaves that lower-powered blowers could only push around. The real constraint is runtime, which can be as short as fifteen minutes at full power, so heavy cleanup needs a second battery.
              </p>
