            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do battery leaf blowers run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Realistically 15 to 40 minutes depending on power setting and battery size. Running at full power or in turbo mode drains a large pack in roughly fifteen minutes, while working at a moderate variable-speed setting can extend that to forty minutes or more. Most yard work does not require full power, so speed control makes a large difference.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a backpack blower worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If your cleanup regularly takes more than half an hour, yes. The harness transfers weight to your hips and shoulders, which removes the arm fatigue that limits handheld sessions to twenty or thirty minutes, and backpack units carry much larger engines. The trade-offs are cost, storage space, and enough setup friction that you will not grab it for quick jobs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How loud are leaf blowers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Battery blowers typically run around 65 dB at the operator, roughly conversation level, while gas blowers commonly reach 95 dB or more. Because decibels are logarithmic, that 30 dB gap represents a very large difference in perceived loudness and in how far the sound carries. Gas blowers warrant hearing protection; most battery blowers do not.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
