        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Leaf Blower Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most leaf blower frustration comes from buying on the wrong specification or working against yourself once you start.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on MPH Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High air speed with low volume produces a narrow, fast jet that scatters leaves rather than moving a mass of them forward. Manufacturers advertise MPH prominently because it is the more impressive-sounding number. For clearing a lawn of dry leaves, CFM is what determines how fast the job goes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Full Power for Everything</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Full throttle is louder, drains the battery or fuel faster, and blasts mulch and dust out of beds while scattering the leaves you just gathered. Variable speed control exists for a reason, and most yard work is faster at a moderate setting because you keep more control over where material goes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blowing Without a Plan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moving leaves in whichever direction is convenient at the moment creates scattered piles that all have to be consolidated afterward. Decide where the leaves are going before you start, check the wind direction, and work in consistent parallel passes toward that single destination.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Gas Into a Jurisdiction Phasing It Out</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A growing number of municipalities restrict blower hours, cap decibel levels, or ban gas blowers entirely on published timelines. Buying a gas blower you expect to keep for a decade into an area moving that direction is an expensive mistake that a quick check of local ordinances prevents.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

